  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1027 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA low pressure system passing to the north will only bring some\nmid clouds to the region as a weak cold front sweeps through the\nwestern Great Lakes. The atmosphere is too dry for any\nprecipitation to fall from this front as it tracks through today\nand will mainly be marked by a shift in wind direction to a\nwesterly direction. A tight pressure gradient will cause surface\nwinds to gust to 25 to 35 knots (30-40 mph) as the low tracks to \nthe north, which may blow around loose objects.\n\nDry weather continues on Friday as a low pressure system develops\nacross the northern Plains. This low pressure system will bring\nour next chance for rain this weekend as another low developing\nacross the southern Plains lifts northeast and draws in moisture\nfrom the Gulf over the Great Lakes. Chances for measurable\nrainfall are high (80-100%) with a decent chance for at least\n0.50\" of precipitation (30-70%) with the highest chances across\ncentral and north-central Wisconsin. There is even a chance\n(20-40%) for 1 inch of rain or more across central and north-\ncentral Wisconsin with this system given the decent dynamics and\nabundant moisture. The best chances for precipitation still look\nto be Saturday afternoon through Sunday morning with chances \nincreasing from west to east as the system arrives.\n\nCold air wrapping in behind the low as it tracks over Quebec will\nallow snow to mix in on Sunday afternoon then changeover to \nmainly snow Sunday night. Moisture will be limited by this point; \ntherefore, snowfall amounts will be very light. Probabilistic data\nindicates there is a 20-60% chance for measurable snow across far\nnorth-central Wisconsin through Monday morning with the highest \nchances across Vilas County. The chance for an inch or more of \nsnow is 20-40% during this same period and mainly confined to \nVilas County where there will be moisture contribution from the \nLake Superior snowbelt.\n\nWell above normal temperatures will prevail into Saturday, with \nhighs in the 50s to mid 60s (normal highs this time of year are in\nthe upper 30s to lower 40s). Record highs in the upper 50s and \nlow 60s Friday into Saturday look to be in jeopardy, especially \nGreen Bay, Manitowoc and Sturgeon Bay. Several record high \nminimums look to be set on Saturday as well, with Friday night \nlows only dropping into upper 40s to low 50s, provided it does not\ncool off too fast before midnight. Much cooler air arrives behind\nthe low pressure system next week with highs tumbling in the 30s \nto around 40 and lows in the teens and 20s.",
